From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: a733: add GMAC0 ethernet

Wire the A733's single Gigabit MAC (GMAC0) on the Radxa Cubie A7S: the DWC
Ether-QoS core, its in-block GMAC_CFG "syscon", the RGMII0 pin group on bank
PH, and the external Maxio MAE0621A PHY on MDIO. This is DT-only on top of the
`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-gmac0-clocks` CCU clocks/resets and the `net-stmmac-dwmac-sun55i-a733-syscon-offset` dwmac-sun55i sun60iw2 compatible; no new
driver code here.

Every value is anchored to primary sources (A733 UM, board schematic v1.10, the MAE0621A
datasheet, live-silicon readback), not to the vendor DT:
  - MAC core @0x04500000 size 0x8000 (DWC-EQoS, VERSION 0x2053); the glue
    register block starts at 0x04508000 so the core node must not span it.
  - syscon node @0x04508000: GMAC_CFG lives inside the GMAC block on A733, not
    in system-control -- a separate node the glue reaches via a phandle.
  - IRQ GIC_SPI 172 = GMAC0_TOP.
  - rgmii0_pins = the 14 connected RGMII0 signals on PH at mux 5; PH3 and PH15
    are omitted -- their series resistors R104/R99 are NC on this board, so
    muxing them drives a dead net (schematic v1.10 p15).
  - PHY at MDIO addr 1: strap PHYAD[2:0]=001 read off the schematic (PHYAD0
    pull-up R116 fitted, PHYAD1/2 pulled down by R117/R118) and silicon-
    confirmed (id 0x7b744412 on the 6 GiB board and the 8 GiB board).
  - PHY reset = PH16 (SoC ball AU2 -> RA4 -> GMAC1_RSTn_L -> R132 -> PHYRSTB),
    active low, on the *mdio bus* node -- see below.
  - phy-mode "rgmii-id": delay added once, at the PHY (Maxio driver), matching
    dwmac-sun55i adding no MAC delay. Derived from driver behaviour, not copied.

The PHY reset sits on the mdio bus node, not on the PHY child, and that is
load-bearing. phylib reads the PHY ID *first* (fwnode_mdio.c, get_phy_device())
and only fetches a child's reset-gpios afterwards, in phy_device_register() ->
mdiobus_register_gpiod(); a child reset can therefore never wake a PHY that is
mute during the scan. Only the bus-level reset in __mdiobus_register()
(mdio_bus_provider.c: assert -> reset-delay-us -> deassert -> reset-post-delay-us)
runs before any child is probed. This matters on this board because PH16 idles
LOW -- its strap divider has the pull-up R1 (47K) unfitted and the pull-down R6
(1K) fitted -- so the PHY comes out of power-on *held in reset* and stays there
until software drives the pin high. boot0 only samples PH16 (DDR-PARA-SEL,
latched long before the kernel); nothing deasserts it for us.

One item stays flagged until a board proves it:
  - the RGMII delay choice (phy-mode) -- validate at a real 1000M link.

The node is status = "okay"; if the Maxio PHY fails to bind it falls back to
the generic PHY (link still comes up), so this cannot brick the boot.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -708,12 +708,36 @@
 				pins = "PC0";
 				allwinner,pinmux = <3>;
 				function = "mmc2";
 				drive-strength = <40>;
 				bias-pull-down;
 			};
+
+			/*
+			 * RGMII0 -> Maxio MAE0621A PHY, all on bank PH at mux 5
+			 * (A733-PINMUX: every RGMII0 signal is selector 0101).
+			 * Exactly the 14 connected RGMII signals are muxed. Two PH
+			 * pins are deliberately left out, both because the board
+			 * depopulates their series resistor (schematic v1.10 p15):
+			 * PH15 = RGMII0-EPHY-25M reaches PHY_XTALOUT only through
+			 * R99 = NC -- the PHY is clocked by its own 25 MHz crystal
+			 * Y1 (load caps C87/C126 fitted), so the SoC-generated
+			 * reference is not used on this board; PH3 = RGMII0-CLKIN
+			 * would take PHY_CLKOUT125 back in through R104 = NC.
+			 * Muxing either one drives a dead net. PH16 (the PHY reset)
+			 * is a plain GPIO, not part of this mux group.
+			 */
+			rgmii0_pins: rgmii0-pins {
+				pins = "PH0", "PH1", "PH2", "PH4",
+				       "PH5", "PH6", "PH7", "PH8",
+				       "PH9", "PH10", "PH11", "PH12",
+				       "PH13", "PH14";
+				allwinner,pinmux = <5>;
+				function = "gmac0";
+			};
+
 		};
 
 		ccu: clock-controller@2002000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-ccu";
 			reg = <0x0 0x02002000 0x0 0x2000>;
 			clocks = <&osc24M>, <&osc32k>, <&iosc>,
@@ -1331,6 +1355,106 @@
 		};
 
 		/*
+		 * GMAC0 (Ethernet) -- the A733's single Gigabit MAC, a Synopsys
+		 * DWC_ether_qos v5.30 core (MAC_VERSION 0x2053, silicon-read),
+		 * driving RJ45 J2 through an external Maxio MAE0621A RGMII PHY.
+		 * Handled by mainline dwmac-sun55i (the GMAC200 glue); the
+		 * "allwinner,sun60iw2-gmac200" compatible and its in-block syscon
+		 * offset are added by patch `net-stmmac-dwmac-sun55i-a733-syscon-offset`. The core region is 0x8000
+		 * (0x04500000..0x04507FFF); the Allwinner glue register GMAC_CFG
+		 * (RGMII mode/delay/PHY-select) is the separate syscon node below
+		 * at 0x04508000 -- on A733 that register lives in the GMAC's own
+		 * block, not in system-control, so the glue references it by a
+		 * "syscon" phandle. IRQ = GMAC0_TOP GIC_SPI 172.
+		 *
+		 * phy-mode = "rgmii-id": all RGMII clock delay is added at the PHY.
+		 * The Maxio driver's config_init programs BOTH the TX and RX MMD
+		 * delay for -id (it keys off phydev->interface), and dwmac-sun55i
+		 * adds no MAC-side delay unless tx/rx-internal-delay-ps is set (it
+		 * is not) -- so delay lives in one place, no double count. This is
+		 * derived from how the two drivers actually behave, NOT copied from
+		 * the vendor DT (which used "rgmii" + MAC glue delays TX=12/RX=10).
+		 * It MUST still be validated at a real gigabit link: if 1000M is
+		 * unstable this is the first knob to turn.
+		 *
+		 */
+		gmac0: ethernet@4500000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60iw2-gmac200",
+				     "snps,dwmac-5.30a";
+			reg = <0x0 0x04500000 0x0 0x8000>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 172 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			interrupt-names = "macirq";
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_GMAC0>, <&ccu CLK_MBUS_GMAC0>;
+			clock-names = "stmmaceth", "mbus";
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_GMAC0>, <&ccu RST_BUS_GMAC0_AXI>;
+			reset-names = "stmmaceth", "ahb";
+			syscon = <&gmac0_syscon>;
+			pinctrl-names = "default";
+			pinctrl-0 = <&rgmii0_pins>;
+			phy-mode = "rgmii-id";
+			phy-handle = <&ext_rgmii_phy>;
+			phy-supply = <&reg_swout1>;
+			status = "okay";
+
+			/*
+			 * PHY reset lives on the BUS node, not on the PHY child.
+			 * phylib reads the PHY ID first (fwnode_mdio.c,
+			 * get_phy_device()) and only fetches a child's reset-gpios
+			 * later, in phy_device_register() -> mdiobus_register_gpiod()
+			 * -- so a child reset can never wake a PHY that is mute
+			 * during the scan. Only the bus reset in __mdiobus_register()
+			 * (mdio_bus_provider.c: assert -> reset-delay-us -> deassert
+			 * -> reset-post-delay-us) runs before the children probe.
+			 *
+			 * PH16 = PHYRSTB, active low, schematic v1.10: SoC ball AU2
+			 * -> RA4 (0R, fitted) -> GMAC1_RSTn_L -> R132 -> PHYRSTB.
+			 * The pin idles LOW -- the strap divider has the pull-up R1
+			 * (47K) unfitted and the pull-down R6 (1K) fitted -- so the
+			 * PHY is held in reset from power-on until software drives
+			 * PH16 high. boot0 only samples the pin (DDR-PARA-SEL strap,
+			 * latched long before the kernel), it never deasserts it, so
+			 * this reset is what brings the PHY up at all. Timing from
+			 * the MAE0621A datasheet: assert >= 10 ms, >= 72 ms settle
+			 * before register access.
+			 */
+			mdio {
+				compatible = "snps,dwmac-mdio";
+				#address-cells = <1>;
+				#size-cells = <0>;
+				reset-gpios = <&pio 7 16 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+				reset-delay-us = <10000>;
+				reset-post-delay-us = <100000>;
+
+				/*
+				 * Maxio MAE0621A at MDIO address 0x01: strap
+				 * PHYAD[2:0]=001, read off schematic v1.10 p15
+				 * (PHYAD0 pull-up R116 4K7 fitted / R115 NC,
+				 * PHYAD1 pull-down R117, PHYAD2 pull-down R118)
+				 * and silicon-confirmed -- the PHY ACKs at :01
+				 * with id 0x7b744412 on the 6 GiB board and the 8 GiB board.
+				 */
+				ext_rgmii_phy: ethernet-phy@1 {
+					compatible = "ethernet-phy-ieee802.3-c22";
+					reg = <1>;
+				};
+			};
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * GMAC0 glue "syscon" = the single Allwinner GMAC_CFG register
+		 * (RGMII mode / TX-RX delay / PHY-select). On A733 it is the first
+		 * register of the GMAC glue block at 0x04508000 -- a node separate
+		 * from the MAC core (which ends at 0x04507FFF), referenced by the
+		 * dwmac-sun55i glue via the "syscon" phandle. The sun60iw2 match
+		 * data (patch `net-stmmac-dwmac-sun55i-a733-syscon-offset`) makes the glue write at offset 0x00 of this
+		 * regmap (vs 0x34 on the A523, whose register is in system-control).
+		 */
+		gmac0_syscon: syscon@4508000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60iw2-gmac-syscon", "syscon";
+			reg = <0x0 0x04508000 0x0 0x1000>;
+		};
+
+		/*
 		 * AON / CPUS domain. The R-CCU (PRCM @0x07010000) provides the AON
 		 * bus clocks; the r_pio (S_GPIO @0x07025000) is the AON pin bank
 		 * PL/PM. Base + geometry from the pin tables of the A733 user manual (PL @0x00/14 pins, PM @0x30/
